  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Monday)...\n\nA 500 mb ridge over the plains and a trough over western and\ncentral Montana is creating a favorable environment for \nthunderstorms across much of the northern plains through Tuesday.\nThe 700 mb troughing and thermal ridge will combine to advect Gulf\nmoisture into the Northern Plains leading to Dewpoints in the 50s\nF and an axis of instability (500-1,000 J/KG CAPE). Starting this\nafternoon and evening, the upper level trough will create a \nregion of >60 kts of 0-6 km shear combined with a 25-30 kt low \nlevel jet increasing low level helicity. These ingredients will be\nin place this evening and overnight for far southeast Montana \n(Carter and Fallon Counties). 0-6 km shear is expected to be \nperpendicular to forcing leading to a likely isolated discrete \nconvection mode with hail (>1 inch) being the biggest threat. \n\nBy Tuesday, the 500 mb trough will have moved slightly to the \neast pushing the best 0-6 km shear into the Dakotas. Forcing will \nlikely be stronger Tuesday however with convection developing \nalong a cold front in an environment with adequate instability \n(~500 J/kg) and shear (40-50 kts). Parallel oriented shear will \nlead to linear convection being more likely. Convection Tuesday is\nexpected to start in the west before the risk shifts to the east \nin the evening and overnight. The highest risk for severe storms \nwill once again be in southeast Montana (Carter, Fallon, Powder \nRiver, and Custer Counties) where hail in excess of 1 inch and \nwinds in excess of 60 mph will be the main threats. \n\nAnother wave moves across the region Thursday bringing additional\nshower and thunderstorm chances (50-70%). Far southeastern \nMontana has the best chance at seeing severe thunderstorms \nThursday. Temperatures increase throughout the week with 70s F \nthrough Thursday increasing into the 80s and low 90s F Friday \nthrough the weekend. An upper level trough to our west brings \nprecipitation chances (30-50%) for Sunday and Monday. Not much \nmore is known about this system at this time. Torgerson",
